1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:05,300
Sorry, Robbie. Sorry to be late this morning,

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:08,900
but, well, we've still got some time for a cup of coffee.

3
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:11,150
I can't wait to see my old pal Charley Rafer.

4
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:25,300
Neither can I.

5
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:27,000
So you thought about it, huh?

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,300
Yes, I have, Dad.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:29,860
Well, I'm glad.

8
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,270
I knew you'd realize

9
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:34,300
that this interview could be an important experience for you.

10
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:36,600
I came to that conclusion.

11
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:39,420
That's very wise, Robbie.

12
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:41,900
Very wise.

13
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,400
Now let's head off for the city and the university club.

14
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,200
Thanks, Dad.

15
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,900
Thanks ... for what?

16
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,730
Thanks for hearing me out. And ...

17
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:53,200
And ...?

18
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,900
And thanks for being such an understanding father.

19
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:13,500
Well, thank you, Robbie. Thank you.

20
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:14,800
Philip Stewart!

21
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,000
It's great to see you!

22
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,600
Charley Rafer--you look as young as ever.

23
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,900
You must be Robbie.

24
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:26,600
Hi.

25
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:29,400
Yes, this is my youngest son Robbie.

26
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:30,900
Robbie, I want you to meet

27
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:34,700
one of the best tennis players on the Michigan team--

28
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:35,800
Charley Rafer.

29
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,320
Nice to meet you, Dean Rafer.

30
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:40,200
Well, are you as good a tennis player as your dad?

31
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,900
No, I'm not very good at it.

32
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:45,000
Frankly, neither was I.

33
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,900
Charley was the star of the team.

34
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:49,300
Yeah. Thanks.

35
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,140
Well, how're you been, Philip?

36
00:01:51,140 --> 00:01:53,500
Oh, working too hard.

37
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:54,700
Doesn't show.

38
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:55,900
How's Ellen?

39
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:58,000
Fine, thank you.

40
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,400
And how's Marge?

41
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:03,400
She's still giving the toughest English history exams

42
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:07,500
in the school and loving every minute of it.

43
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:08,900
And speaking of minutes,

44
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,900
I have interviews until noon,

45
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,900
so why don't we get right to work?

46
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:16,500
Can you have lunch with us later?

47
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:18,000
I'd love to, Philip, but I'm afraid I can't.

48
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,300
I'm only here two days,

49
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:22,100
and I have interviews with twenty-six applicants.

50
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:23,400
I understand.

51
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,600
Well, thanks.

52
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:28,220
I'll wait outside.

53
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,100
Good luck, Son.

54
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:31,600
Did you bring your transcript from high school?

55
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,300
Yes, sir.

56
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:33,410
Right here.

57
00:02:33,410 --> 00:02:35,890
Thank you.

58
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:41,800
Please sit down.

59
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,100
I see under "activities" that

60
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:44,900
you've been writing for the school paper.

61
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:46,700
Yes, sir.

62
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:48,500
What kinds of articles have you written?

63
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,200
All kinds--sports, editorials, theater reviews.

64
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,900
You name it, I've written it.

65
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,500
Hmmm.

66
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,400
Well, have you ever thought of becoming a journalist?

67
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,000
A professional writer?

68
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,500
Not until recently.

69
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:06,200
Michigan has a fine School of Journalism.

70
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:09,500
Yes, I know that.

71
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,000
You seem to have some reservations.

72
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:16,100
I'm a little uncertain.

73
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:18,100
It's been very nice talking to you.

74
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:20,600
Nice talking to you, sir.

75
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,300
One piece of advice.

76
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:27,800
The most important thing is for you to decide your own future.

77
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,500
Yes, sir.

78
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,600
Good-bye, Dean Rafer.

79
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:33,600
Good-bye, Robbie. Good luck.
